</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Model versions: Deepseek-v3 (25.03.24), GPT-4o (24.05.13), Claude-3-7-Sonnet (25.02.19), LLaMA-4-Scout (25.04.05), Gemini-2.5-Pro-Preview (25.03.25).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental evaluation on 50 natural language command tasks demonstrates that the proposed coordination field agentic system outperforms all baseline models across multiple key performance metrics as shown in Table 1. Specifically, it achieves the highest TPA of 96%, significantly exceeding GPT-4o (74%)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, Claude-3-7-Sonnet (76%)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and Deepseek-v3 (70%)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, indicating its superior semantic understanding and robustness in converting unstructured commands into executable UAV instructions. In terms of CE, the system maintains an average rate of 95%, consistently outperforming other models such as GPT-4o (85%) and LLaMA-4-Scout (79%)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and remains highly reliable even under task-burst scenarios with over 80% effective coverage. With respect to TLB, our method demonstrates the lowest task distribution deviation of only 0.8, compared to 2.1 and 1.6 observed in Deepseek-v3 and Claude-3-7-Sonnet respectively, which significantly reduces resource clustering and enhances spatial coordination. Additionally, the UUR reaches 97%, confirming that the majority of UAVs remain actively engaged in task execution, whereas competing models such as GPT-4o and LLaMA-4-Scout fall below 85%. These results collectively validate the system’s strong coordination capability, dynamic adaptability, and overall efficiency in managing heterogeneous UAV swarms within complex urban environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper proposes a coordination field Agentic system for UAV task coordination. The system demonstrates efficiency, stability, and adaptability, enabling real-time parsing of natural language instructions and efficient task allocation via potential fields, fluid-based velocity fields, and vortex-based guidance. Experimental results verify its superior performance in complex and dynamic urban environments. In future work, we plan to extend the system to three-dimensional scenarios and real-world UAV swarms.</p>
</div>
</section>
